'''essentially, it seems that arrays are made for mathematical operations while lists are not. to do simple arithmetic arrays are the better choice as they
can be manipulated much easier than lists can. We have to use loops and other operations when with arrays all we have to do is use the operater (+, -, *, /)

'''





# Your code here

def array_return(arr1):
    second_value = [arr1[0, 1], arr1[1, 1], arr1[2, 1]]
    return second_value

arr1 = np.array([[1, 2, 3,], [10, 20, 30], [100, 200, 300]])
print(array_return(arr1))





# Your comment here

# I looked at the NumPy Array Indexing part to find how to pull the second value, and the Access 2-D Arrays to find how to pull values from different rows and columns





# I would create a new list with the values being sample_list[0,1], and iterate the first number from 0 - n with n being the amount of nested lists 
# (ie the amount of rows)

def list_return(new_list):
    second_value = [row[1] for row in new_list if len(row) > 1]
    #checking for only rows with 2 or more indices, and  find the second value in the row. 
    return second_value

new_list = ([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6])
print(list_return(new_list))





